## Proof

We must show that for all $f, g \in \operatorname{Hom}(C, M)$,

$$h_*(f \nabla g) = h_*(f) \nabla h_*(g).$$

**Compute the left-hand side.** By definition of $\nabla$ on $\operatorname{Hom}(C, M)$ and of $h_*$,

$$h_*(f \nabla g) = h \circ (f \nabla g) = h \circ \mu_M \circ \langle f, g \rangle.$$

**Compute the right-hand side.** By definition of $\nabla$ on $\operatorname{Hom}(C, N)$ and of $h_*$,

$$h_*(f) \nabla h_*(g) = \mu_N \circ \langle h_*(f),\, h_*(g) \rangle = \mu_N \circ \langle h \circ f,\, h \circ g \rangle.$$

**Key identity.** The universal property of the product (the pairing $\langle -, - \rangle$) gives

$$\langle h \circ f,\, h \circ g \rangle = (h \times h) \circ \langle f, g \rangle,$$

since both sides are the unique morphism $C \to N \times N$ whose composites with the two projections are $h \circ f$ and $h \circ g$ respectively. Substituting,

$$h_*(f) \nabla h_*(g) = \mu_N \circ (h \times h) \circ \langle f, g \rangle.$$

**Apply the magma morphism hypothesis.** Since $h$ is a magma morphism, $h \circ \mu_M = \mu_N \circ (h \times h)$. Therefore

$$h_*(f \nabla g) = \underbrace{(h \circ \mu_M)}_{=\,\mu_N \circ (h \times h)} \circ \langle f, g \rangle = \mu_N \circ (h \times h) \circ \langle f, g \rangle = h_*(f) \nabla h_*(g).$$

Thus $h_*(f \nabla g) = h_*(f) \nabla h_*(g)$ for all $f, g \in \operatorname{Hom}(C, M)$, so $h_*: \operatorname{Hom}(C, M) \to \operatorname{Hom}(C, N)$ is a magma homomorphism. $\blacksquare$

